+//! Rolling a cluster one node at a time, without ever losing the majority.
+//!
+//! `meshkv/5.15-cluster-drills` is the operator's half of `5.14`: a partition is something that
+//! *happens* to a cluster, and a rolling restart is something an operator *does* to one — deploy a
+//! new binary, rotate a certificate, replace a machine. The rule is the same arithmetic as every
+//! majority in this track, applied to a schedule instead of a fault:
+//!
+//! > at every instant of the restart, a majority of the cluster has to be up.
+//!
+//! A three-node cluster can lose one node; a five-node cluster can lose two. A plan that takes down
+//! the second node before the first is back is a plan that stops the cluster, and it is a plan that
+//! looks perfectly reasonable in a spreadsheet.
+//!
+//! So the plan is a **value**, and it is checked before it is run:
+//!
+//! * the order is the operator's (or the seed's), but the *quorum* check is arithmetic;
+//! * a node that is already down does not have to come back for the plan to continue — but it does
+//!   count as down, and the plan refuses the moment down nodes would be a majority;
+//! * a node that comes back is *back*: the plan tracks which nodes are up, so a plan that restarts
+//!   the same node twice without a failure in between is a no-op the check refuses.
+//!
+//! The last one is what makes the plan honest: a restart that does not change anything is a step
+//! somebody typed by mistake, and a plan that accepted it would report "restarted" for a cluster that
+//! never went down.
+
