+# Rolling restarts: one node at a time, and the arithmetic that says why
+
+A rolling restart is what an operator does to a cluster that has to keep serving: deploy a new
+binary, rotate a certificate, replace a machine. The rule fits in one sentence, and it is the same
+arithmetic every majority in this project has used:
+
+> **At every instant of the restart, a majority of the cluster has to be up.**
+
+A three-node cluster can lose one node. A five-node cluster can lose two. A plan that takes down the
+second node before the first is back is a plan that stops the cluster — and it is a plan that looks
+perfectly reasonable in a spreadsheet, which is why it is a value here and not a paragraph.
+
+## The plan
+
+A plan is an order over the cluster's members. It is checked before it runs:
+
+- **every member exactly once.** A plan that misses a node, names a stranger, or restarts one twice
+  does not describe the cluster it claims to;
+- **the majority at every step.** The plan walks its order, taking each node down and bringing it
+  back before the next, and refuses the step that would leave fewer than `⌊n/2⌋ + 1` nodes up;
+- **a node that is already down counts.** Its turn brings it back (the timeline says it was not up),
+  but it also counts as down for every later step's majority.
+
+## What the timeline is for
+
+The plan answers a timeline rather than a yes/no: one entry per step, naming the node, whether it
+was up before its turn, and whether the majority held. That is what a runbook prints, and what an
+operator reads at 3 a.m. — "n2, was up, majority held" is a sentence somebody can act on, where
+"restart ok" is not.
+
+## Why a schedule is worth a value
+
+Because the interesting failures are *plans*, not code. A cluster that stops during a deploy is
+almost never a bug in the restart — it is a plan that was one node too greedy, or one that assumed a
+node that was down was up. Making the plan a value means the check runs before the deploy, on the
+machine, with the same arithmetic the cluster uses to decide anything.
